  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/b4bd77dce6f600ed55fd3443cca91ae3f95b052e38d451334decc0f0b85dd94e.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s∥t.$",
  "worked_solution": "Consider $∠1$ and $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/259f366823da9de4543e0e245ec1186ac8f50e1a53616290e76271f9672a92f4.png)\n\nNote that $∠1$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠1=2x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is $5x+{5}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2=5x+{5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14178066/assets/10629276463e5cf33b4a0c5b0ba1237eec1201fe142e644628f0a6a96fa54c32.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and the third angle whose measure is ${70}^{∘}$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+(5x+{5}^{∘})+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={105}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{{105}^{∘}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & ={15}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
